alternative spellings across the FOUR        : 0

VERDICT A: F157-10 stands as literally stated. No spelling of a width
           universal appears in the four files. The knife does not cut.
           BUT its instrument does not enumerate the spellings, and 109
           uses one of them (2 times, at 109:156 and 109:381).
           So the CONTROL that F157-10 carries -- 'threads any and
           tfeat any are non-zero, so the zero is the corpus' -- proves
           the word 'any' is findable, not that the width axis's
           spellings were searched. Those are different conjuncts.
           And 157's prose generalises to 'topic five's corpus', which
           includes 109, where the sweeping claim is false: 109's very
           first predicate is a universal over integer width.

=== B. 154 audited on the same terms, by its author ===
  findings stated              : 17
  carrying `W any`             : 0
  carrying another spelling    : 0
  carrying `W in 1..=64`       : 3   (exhaustive over a u64 container)
  carrying `threads any`       : 0
  carrying `target features any`: 0
  carrying `threads = 1` (fixed) : 13
  predicate blocks found        : 18

CONTROL the extractor can read 154's predicate blocks: 18 blocks, 13 carrying threads = 1

VERDICT B: 154 carries `W any` zero times, so it is a fifth instance of
           F157-10's pattern and 157 did not count it. Three of its
           findings carry W in 1..=64, which is exhaustive over every
           width a u64 container holds rather than a three-width sample.

           And 154 carries NO `any` on ANY axis: not width, not threads,
           not target features. Read literally under I13 its findings hold
           at threads = 1, at one host's baseline features, at the widths
           listed, and nowhere else. That is what was measured, so the
           recording is honest; whether the notation should force a
           measured-everywhere finding to say so is Q65 and is op's.
